        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function",
        "problem": "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}+{c}_{1},\\,{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3},\\,{e}^{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration.",
