      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-99350",
        "knowledge_point_id": "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8",
        "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function",
        "problem": "Find $\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$",
        "worked_solution": "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\text{d}t=⟨\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x(t)={e}^{t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\text{d}t={e}^{t}+{c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,$ we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u=2t.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)+{c}_{2} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨{e}^{t}+{c}_{1},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
