        "knowledge_point": "Determining the Position of a Particle Given Its Velocity and Initial Position",
        "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=9,$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$",
        "worked_solution": "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 9\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(1)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =9(1)+C \\\\ C & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=9t-9.\n$$",
